    "habit": {
      "word": "HABIT",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "The usual condition or state of a person or thing, either natural or acquired, regarded as something had, possessed, and firmly retained; as, a religious habit; his habit is morose; elms have a spreading habit; esp., physical temperament or constitution; as, a full habit of body.",
          "partOfSpeech": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "(Biol.)",
          "partOfSpeech": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "The general appearance and manner of life of a living organism.",
          "partOfSpeech": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "Fixed or established custom; ordinary course of conduct; practice; usage; hence, prominently, the involuntary tendency or aptitude to perform certain actions which is acquired by their frequent repetition; as, habit is second nature; also, peculiar ways of acting; characteristic forms of behavior. A man of very shy, retired habits. W. Irving.",
          "partOfSpeech": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "Outward appearance; attire; dress; hence, a garment; esp., a closely fitting garment or dress worn by ladies; as, a riding habit. Costly thy habit as thy purse can buy. Shak. There are, among the states, several of Venus, in different habits. Addison. do easily, naturally, and with growing certainty, what we do often; custom is external, being habitual use or the frequent repetition of the same act. The two operate reciprocally on each other. The custom of giving produces a habit of liberality; habits of devotion promote the custom of going to church. Custom also supposes an act of the will, selecting given modes of procedure; habit is a law of our being, a kind of \"second nature\" which grows up within us. How use doth breed a habit in a man ! Shak. He who reigns . . . upheld by old repute, Consent, or custom. Milton.",
          "partOfSpeech": ""
        },
        {
          "text": "To inhabit. [Obs.] In thilke places as they [birds] habiten. Rom. of R.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To dress; to clothe; to array. They habited themselves lite those rural deities. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To accustom; to habituate. [Obs.] Chapman.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
